WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:40.000
This is 99% invisible. I'm Roman Mars. In the 1960s, American cities were in crisis. Infrastructure was crumbling. Traffic and pollution were terrible. Crime was up. Cities weren't particularly nice places to live. White people were able to flee urban centers for the suburbs thanks to federal help with mortgages and new freeway development. That process would come to be known as White Flight. Meanwhile, black populations in cities were dealing with housing discrimination and police brutality. Riots were breaking out in cities all over the country, and this awful time was referred
这里是 99% Invisible。我是罗曼·马尔斯。在 20世纪 60 年代，美国城市正处于危机之中。基础设施不断崩溃。交通和污染问题极其严重。犯罪率上升。城市并不是什么特别宜居的地方。由于联邦政府在抵押贷款和新高速公路建设方面提供的帮助，白人得以从城市中心逃往郊区。这一过程后来被称为“白人群飞”。与此同时，城市里的黑人人口正在应对住房歧视和警察暴力。骚乱在全国各地的城市爆发，这段糟糕的时期被

2
00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:12.000
to by scholars as the urban crisis. That's our own Katie Mingo. And the civil rights movement was happening in parallel to the urban crisis, but it wasn't really addressing the problems for black people in cities. The overarching question was, can the cities be saved? How can we make urban life better for people, for all people? That's Roger Biles, professor of history at Illinois State University.
学者们称为城市危机。那是我们的凯蒂·明戈。民权运动与城市危机同时发生，但它并没有真正解决城市中黑人的问题。核心问题是：城市能被拯救吗？我们该如何为人们——为所有人——改善城市生活？那是罗杰·拜尔斯，伊利诺伊州立大学历史学教授。

3
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:53.000
And, you know, there were some folks who looked at the situation and just threw up their hands and said, you know, we just can't salvage what's here. The answer is starting over again. In other words, urban planners thought maybe the problems facing cities were too big and too complicated to fix. And it's within this context of the urban crisis and the civil rights movement that the federal government would come to consider an idea like Seoul City. Floyd McKissick offered an idea today, a new city to be built in the country in North Carolina to be called Seoul City and to be populated mainly
而且，你知道的，有一些人看着这种情况，直接双手一摊，说道：“你知道的，我们简直无法挽回这里的现状。唯一的办法就是重新开始。”换句话说，城市规划者认为，城市面临的问题可能太大、太复杂而无法解决。正是在这种城市危机和民权运动的背景下，联邦政府开始考虑像“灵魂城”这样的构想。弗洛伊德·麦基西克今天提出一个构想：在北卡罗来纳州的乡村建立一座新城市，名为“灵魂城”，他表示，这座城市将主要

4
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:29.000
or entirely by Negroes he says would move there from the ghettos. This tape is from a 1969 NBC National News broadcast and this new city was being proposed by a civil rights leader named Floyd McKissick. In establishing our new city in North Carolina, we will create new jobs and new careers for black people. As a response to the urban crisis, the federal government had announced plans to provide financing to about a dozen brand new towns and Floyd McKissick wanted Seoul City to be one of them.
或全部由那些他所说的将从贫民窟搬迁过去的黑人组成。这段录音摘自 1969 年 NBC 全国新闻广播，这座新城市是由一位名叫弗洛伊德·麦基西克的民权领袖提出的。“通过在北卡罗来纳州建立我们的新城，我们将为黑人创造新的工作和新的职业。”作为对城市危机的一种回应，联邦政府曾宣布计划为大约十几个崭新的城镇提供资金支持，而弗洛伊德·麦基西克希望“灵魂城”能成为其中之一。

5
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:59.000
In this new town, persons will be able to control their own destinies. We have the power to actually be a part of building a town. Come on, it's the story of America. And to be a part of it. That's Jane Ballgroom. She was working as a secretary from a chisic in Harlem when he first started developing the idea for Soul City, which would be a place built for and by black people, a land of black opportunity in rural North Carolina.
在这个新城镇里，人们将能够掌控自己的命运。我们有力量真正参与到城镇的建设中来。来吧，这就是美国的故事。并且成为其中的一部分。那是简·鲍尔格鲁姆。当麦基西克最初在哈莱姆开始构思“灵魂城”这个项目时，她曾在那里担任秘书。“灵魂城”将是一个专为黑人打造、由黑人建立的地方，是北卡罗来纳州乡村的一片黑人机遇之地。

6
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Jane says going to Soul City was like being a pilgrim. You know, we had our own Mayflower ship, we came down in cars. You know, it was the same dog I'm saying. But, you know, maybe not so, the children's had a really hard. The pilgrims did have it really hard, but this wouldn't be easy either. The government would eventually come to support this project, but there would be politics and compromises and very strange bedfellows. And that was all before breaking ground on Soul City. When Floyd McKissick first pitched the idea of an all-black town, there was one
简说，去“灵魂城”就像是一场朝圣。“你知道的，我们有自己的五月花号船，我们是坐汽车南下的。你知道的，我说的是同一个道理。但是，你知道，也许并非如此，孩子们真的过得很艰难。”朝圣者的确过得很艰难，但这也不会轻松。政府最终会支持这个项目，但其中会有政治博弈、妥协以及非常奇怪的政治同盟。而这一切都发生在“灵魂城”破土动工之前。当弗洛伊德·麦基西克首次提出这个全黑人城镇的想法时，有一个

7
00:03:32.000 --> 00:04:10.000
question he got asked a lot. There's a building in a city like this, four-mpire of the trend towards separatism. This is a reporter at an early press conference and a lot of people were curious about this. With Soul City a departure from the idea that black people and white people should integrate together in society? Was this instead black people starting their own thing separate from white people? And you can hear mccasics struggle to answer. Now I think it does the same thing as the Chinese have done in New York. They built them Chinese area, Chinatown.
问题他经常被问到。“这在像这样的城市里建立，是否会助长分裂主义的趋势？”这是一位记者在早期新闻发布会上提出的问题，很多人对此很好奇。“‘灵魂城’是否背离了黑人和白人在社会中应当共同融合的理念？这反而是黑人开创脱离白人的独立事业吗？”你能听到麦基西克在艰难地回答。“现在我认为这和中国人在纽约做的事情是一样的。他们建造了中国城，华人区。”

8
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:45.000
It's a beautiful section of the city that I admire. To understand all the ways that this question about separatism was relevant, you have to understand a bit about who Floyd McKissick was. This photograph here would kill my father, I'm the left here hand in hand with Dr. King. This is Floyd McKissick the second. He's a lawyer and a state senator and he's showing me pictures of his dad that are hanging in his office in Durham, North Carolina. Three of the famous Mark and Washington, where Dr. King gave his eye at a dream speech.
“那是城市中一个美丽的部分，我很赞赏。”为了理解关于这种分裂主义的种种质疑为何切中要害，你必须对弗洛伊德·麦基西克其人有一定了解。“这张照片真的让我父亲……我左边这位正和马丁·路德·金博士手挽着手。”这是小弗洛伊德·麦基西克。他是一名律师兼州参议员，他正在他位于北卡罗来纳州达勒姆的办公室里向我展示挂在那里的他父亲的照片。“华盛顿大游行三杰之一，金博士在那里发表了‘我有一个梦想’的演讲。”

9
00:04:45.000 --> 00:05:13.000
My father and Dr. King led that mark that day. A few years after that march, Floyd McKissick's senior would become the executive director of an organization called CORE. That's the Congress on racial equality. And he was part of a handful of really important civil rights leaders in the 60s, along with people like Dr. King, Roy Wilkins, and Anna Blacepie, Whitney Young of the Urban League, and Stokely Carmichael of SNCC.
“我父亲和金博士那天领导了那场游行。”那次游行几年后，老弗洛伊德·麦基西克成为了一个名为 CORE 的组织的执行主任。也就是种族平等大会。他是 60 年代少数几位真正重要的民权领袖之一，与金博士、罗伊·威尔金斯、阿纳·布莱斯皮（译者注：此处为人名误读组合，通常指小阿瑟·邦内尔/厄尔·布朗等，但按音频多指城市联盟的惠特尼·杨以及 SNCC 的斯托克利·卡迈克尔）、城市联盟的惠特尼·杨以及 SNCC 的斯托克利·卡迈克尔等人齐名。

10
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:47.000
SNCC being the student nonviolent coordinating committee. Because of these leaders and the members of their organizations, huge legal gains were made in civil rights and integration in the 50s and 60s. And then in 1966 there's this kind of pivotal moment in the movement. A civil rights activist named James Meredith is shot by a white gunman while leading a march in Mississippi. Stokely Carmichael and Floyd McKissick both go down in Mississippi to continue the march. Carmichael is arrested and when he gets out of jail he makes a speech where he says, don't be afraid, don't be ashamed, we want black power. We want black power.
SNCC 即学生非暴力协调委员会。正是由于这些领袖及其组织成员的努力，50 和 60 年代在民权和种族融合方面取得了巨大的法律成果。然后在 1966 年，运动中迎来了这样一个关键时刻。一位名叫詹姆斯·梅雷迪思的民权活动家在密西西比州领导游行时被一名白人枪手击中。斯托克利·卡迈克尔和弗洛伊德·麦基西克双双前往密西西比州继续游行。卡迈克尔被捕了，当他出狱时，他发表了一场演讲，其中说道：“不要害怕，不要羞愧，我们要黑人权。我们要黑人权。”

11
00:05:47.000 --> 00:06:04.000
We want black power. We want black power. We have stayed here and we beg the president. We beg the federal government. That's all we've been doing, begging, begging. It's time we stand up and take over. On that day in 1966, the president was going to be the president.
“我们要黑人权。我们要黑人权。我们一直留在这里，我们乞求总统。我们乞求联邦政府。这就是我们一直在做的，乞求，乞求。是时候我们站起来接管一切了。”在 1966 年的那一天，总统将成为……

12
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:25.000
He was going to be the president. He was going to be the president, we beg the federal government. That's all we've been doing, begging, begging, is time we stand up and take over. On that day in 1966, the Black Power Movement was born. And the mechistic of core and Carmichael of SNCC become two of the most vocal advocates of this new movement. What do we need, Black Power? When do we need it now? Stoking my dad were probably the two
他将成为总统。他将成为总统，我们乞求联邦政府。这就是我们一直在做的事，乞求，乞求，是时候我们站起来接管一切了。在 1966 年的那一天，黑人权力运动诞生了。CORE 的麦基西克和 SNCC 的卡迈克尔成为了这一新运动最直言不讳的倡导者。我们需要什么？黑人权力！我们什么时候需要？现在！斯托克利和我爸爸可能是阐明

13
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:57.000
leaders in articulating what that meant. For Carmichael and McKissick, black power was a step further than civil rights and integration. It was essentially the idea that black people should control their own communities, should have proportional representation in government. Again, Stokely Carmichael in 1966. We have to do what every group in this country did. We got to take over the communities where we outnumber people so we can have decent jobs. So we can have decent houses. So we can have decent roads.
这意味着什么的两大领袖。对于卡迈克尔和麦基西克来说，黑人权力比民权和种族融合更进了一步。它本质上的理念是：黑人应该控制自己的社区，应该在政府中拥有按比例的代表权。再次引用 1966 年斯托克利·卡迈克尔的话。“我们必须做这个国家每一个群体都做过的事。我们要接管那些我们人口占优势的社区，这样我们就能拥有体面的工作。这样我们就能拥有体面的住房。这样我们就能拥有体面的道路。”

14
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:39.000
So we can have decent schools. So we can have decent justice. So we can have decent justice. So, McKissick and Carmichael both very much believed in the idea of black power. But in the late 60s, they began to diverge about how to achieve it. Carmichael and many others in the black power movement thought that capitalism was an inherently exploitative system that would always leave someone at the bottom. On the other hand, McKissick began to believe that capitalism was the answer. McKissick actually associates himself with a very important tendency in black power.
“这样我们就能拥有体面的学校。这样我们就能拥有体面的司法。这样我们就能拥有体面的司法。”所以，麦基西克和卡迈克尔都非常笃信黑人权力的理念。但在 60 年代后期，他们在如何实现这一目标上开始出现分歧。卡迈克尔和黑人权力运动中的许多其他人认为，资本主义本质上是一种剥削制度，总会把某些人踩在社会底层。另一方面，麦基西克开始相信资本主义才是答案。麦基西克实际上将自己与黑人权力中一个非常重要的倾向联系在了一起。

15
00:07:39.000 --> 00:08:31.000
That Sundiara Chajua, professor of history and African American studies at the University of Illinois or Banna Champaign, and the tendency he's talking about is one toward black capitalism. What it means, essentially, is that black people should own and control their own capitalist enterprises, and it comes to suggest that business ownership is to path to equality, freedom, justice, social transformation. Too long ago we attempted to divide economics from politics. The question that becomes paramount in say 1970 is a strategy not principle. That's Floyd McKissick, an archival interview with political writer Walter DeVries. We see the subject is becoming intensely pragmatic to us. Absolutely. Absolutely.
伊利诺伊大学厄巴纳-香槟分校的历史与非裔美国人研究教授孙迪亚拉·查朱如说道，他所指的这种倾向就是黑人资本主义。其本质含义是，黑人应该拥有并控制自己的资本企业，这也暗示了企业所有权通往平等、自由、正义和社会变革的道路。曾几何时，我们试图将经济学与政治学割裂开来。比如说在 1970 年，至关重要的问题变成了一种策略而非原则。这是弗洛伊德·麦基西克在与政治作家沃尔特·德弗里斯的档案访谈中的言论。“我们看到这个主题对我们变得具有强烈的实用主义色彩。绝对是的。绝对是的。”

16
00:08:31.000 --> 00:09:03.000
As you've got it with the ideological and the ideological. You don't have to call it pragmatic. You might lose everything that you've gained, all those gains that you've made during the 60s. McKissick believed that the time for idealism was over. It was time for black people to claim their piece of the American pie. Now where the hell are you and what the where, what are you going to do to become a fool fled America? Do you want to go back to Africa? I for one believe I will stay here. Actually, Stokely Carmichael did go to Africa.
“正如你所拥有的意识形态和意识形态。你不必称之为实用主义。你可能会失去你所赢得的一切，失去你在 60 年代取得的所有成果。”麦基西克认为，理想主义的时代已经结束。现在是黑人争取属于他们的美国梦的时候了。现在你到底在哪里，你打算怎么做才能成为一个成熟的美国人？你想回非洲吗？我个人相信我会留在这里。事实上，斯托克利·卡迈克尔确实去了非洲。

17
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:29.000
Leaving behind the black Power movement in America, he moved to Guinea, where he dedicated himself to the all African People's Revolutionary Party. And Floyd McKissick did indeed stay in the States. He resigned from core to start a four-profit company called McKissick Enterprises, and set his sights on building Seoul City. The town he hoped would create as much economic opportunity for black Americans as possible.
他抛下了美国的黑人权力运动，搬到了几内亚，在那里投身于泛非人民革命党。而弗洛伊德·麦基西克确实留在了美国。他辞去了 CORE 的职务，创办了一家名为“麦基西克企业”的营利性公司，并将目光投向了建设“灵魂城”。他希望这座城镇能为美籍黑人创造尽可能多的经济机遇。

18
00:09:29.000 --> 00:10:13.000
And we can bring together, you know, the private sector. You can bring together industry government and educational resources to really build a town free of racism. the town free of racism. The building of black towns is an expression of a desire for autonomy. McKissick's soul city wasn't a first for black town building in the US. Quite a few towns were settled in the great plains between 1890 and 1910 by African-American sharecroppers fleeing the south, but these weren't intentionally planned communities with government support. Amazingly in the late 1960s, just as McKissick becomes convinced that building a new town
“我们可以汇聚私营部门的力量。你可以汇聚工业、政府和教育资源，真正建设一个没有种族主义的城镇。”一个没有种族主义的城镇。黑人城镇的建设是对自治渴望的一种表达。麦基西克的“灵魂城”并不是美国黑人城镇建设的首例。在 1890 年至 1910 年间，大平原上有相当多的城镇是由逃离南方的非裔美国佃农建立的，但这些并不是有政府支持的有计划的社区。令人惊奇的是，在 20 世纪 60 年代末，正当麦基西克确信建设新城镇

19
00:10:13.000 --> 00:11:05.000
is the way to achieve black power, the government struggling to deal with the so-called urban crisis announces their program to help finance several new cities as part of the Urban Growth and new Communities Act. The legislation was passed, which provided a process for building these things. That's historian Roger Biles again, and he says these new cities would be meticulously planned in order to avoid the problems of existing cities. We're going to bring all of the knowledge and all of the insights of urban planning to doing it right this time. The program would be managed by the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development, also known as HUD. Private developers could propose ideas to HUD for new towns. If they were approved, the new towns could sell government-backed bonds to investors on Wall Street.
是实现黑人权力的途径时，正艰难应对所谓城市危机的政府宣布了一项新计划，旨在作为《城市发展与新社区法》的一部分，帮助资助几个新城市。该立法获得通过，为建设这些项目提供了流程。历史学家罗杰·拜尔斯再次说道，他表示这些新城镇将被精心规划，以避免现有城市的问题。“我们将运用城市规划的所有知识和远见，这一次要把事情做好。”该计划将由美国住房和城市发展部（也称为 HUD）管理。私人开发商可以向 HUD 提出新城镇的构想。如果获得批准，这些新城镇就可以向华尔街的投资者出售政府担保的债券。

20
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:36.000
And as McKissick starts talking to people within HUD about this all-black town idea, they're like, look, there's no way we're going to approve something that's perceived as being separatist. So McKissick dials back the language he was using to describe Seoul City. If you look at the things that he wrote and said before he feels out to actual applications, right, it's all about, you know, he's talking about building ideal black communities.
正当麦基西克开始与 HUD 内部的人士谈论这个全黑人城镇的想法时，他们的反应是：“听着，我们绝不可能批准任何被视为具有分裂主义色彩的项目。”因此，麦基西克淡化了他用来描述“灵魂城”的语言。如果你看看他在提交实际申请之前所写和所说的话，就会发现，这全都是关于……他谈论的是建设理想的黑人社区。

21
00:11:36.000 --> 00:12:06.000
After he's gone through that process, then the literature is about building middle class communities that are open. McKissick never envisioned a place where white people would be specifically excluded. He did imagine a majority black town with a majority black leadership, but he couldn't really talk about it like that. In politics, concessions must be made. The next big one would come in the form of an important ally.
在他经历了这一流程之后，相关的文献和宣传就变成了关于建设开放的中产阶级社区。麦基西克从未设想过一个明确排挤白人的地方。他确实设想过一个以黑人为主、由黑人领导层主导的城镇，但他实际上不能那样直言不讳。在政治中，必须做出妥协。下一个重大妥协将以一个重要盟友的形式出现。

22
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:38.000
Mckissick understood that to be approved to build one of these new cities, he'd need friends in high places. He first pitches the idea to the current president, Lyndon Johnson. And Johnson seems interested, but then he decides not to run for re-election. And so, in 1968, Mckissick looks at the new field of presidential candidates and asks himself, who is the most viable person to support the Seoul City Project? That's Devon Fergis. He's a professor of African American Studies at Ohio State University.
麦基西克明白，要想获准建设这些新城镇之一，他需要在高层有朋友。他首先向当时的总统林登·约翰逊提出了这个构想。约翰逊似乎很感兴趣，但他随后决定不再竞选连任。因此，在 1968 年，麦基西克审视了新一届总统候选人阵营，并问自己：谁是对支持“灵魂城”项目最切实可行的人选？那是德文·弗格斯。他是俄亥俄州立大学非裔美国人研究教授。

23
00:12:38.000 --> 00:13:20.000
He opts for Richard Nixon. We've had enough of big promises and little action. The time has come for honest government in the United States of America. And it's chocolately. Nixon actually supports this project. Nixon believed in black capitalism because Nixon believed in capitalism. He believed that when people owned property and businesses, they had more of a stake in the country. Apart from that, the Republican Party wanted to bring in black voters. In Republican Party in the late 60s and 70s was a much more big tinted sort of party.
